  "sections": [
    {
      "key": "distribution",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/31-crayfishes-shrimps-crabs-snails-mussels/uca_tangeri_en/",
      "html": "<p>The eastern Atlantic from Portugal to Angola is the origin of the fiddler crab Uca tangeri, where it inhabits sand and mangrove coasts.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "habitat",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/31-crayfishes-shrimps-crabs-snails-mussels/uca_tangeri_en/",
      "html": "<p>In their periodically flooded habitat they inhabit muddy burrows, which they leave at ebb-tide to take in food.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "maintenance",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/31-crayfishes-shrimps-crabs-snails-mussels/uca_tangeri_en/",
      "html": "<p>For maintenance, try to imitate their habitat: the aquarium should have a land part with sand and gravel and a water part filled with brackish water. Some hiding places give them retreat possibilities.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "diet",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/31-crayfishes-shrimps-crabs-snails-mussels/uca_tangeri_en/",
      "html": "<p>As omnivores they are not choosy and feed on nearly any organic matter the sea offers them. In home care, frozen and granulated food can be fed, as well as various fruits and vegetables.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "dimorphism",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/31-crayfishes-shrimps-crabs-snails-mussels/uca_tangeri_en/",
      "html": "<p>The major claw of the males grows to an enormous size. It is used to attract females and in fights between rival males.</p>"
    }
  ],
